      "source": "hn",
      "title": "Show HN: Esp32sim – Run ESP32 firmware in the browser",
      "url": "https://github.com/mikroverk/esp32sim",
      "author": "joakimeriksson",
      "external_id": "50131189",
      "summary": null,
      "why_read": null,
      "authors": [],
      "category": null,
      "published": "2026-10-17T13:51:08Z",
      "score": 173,
      "comments": 32,
      "hn_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50131189",
      "snippet": "Hi HN,<p>I’ve been working on esp32sim, an open-source ESP32 emulator that runs in the browser.<p>The fun part of the demo is that there is no ESP32 involved — the firmware is executing in the emulator.<p>The goal is to make ESP32 software easier to experiment with, test and simulate without always needing physical hardware connected.<p>Source:\n<a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;mikroverk&#x2F;esp32sim\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;mikroverk&#x2F;esp32sim</a><p>I made a short video showing it in action:\n<a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;youtu.be&#x2F;waoWFilTWEc\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;youtu.be&#x2F;waoWFilTWEc</a><p>I’m particularly interested in feedback from embedded developers: what would an emulator like this need to support before you’d actually use it for development, testing or CI?",
      "links": []
    },
